movement across submarkets. Ahead, two new projects scheduled for completion in Q4 will add 27,100 pings of space.\nL220: \nL221: \nL222: \nL223: 【111†Click here to download the report†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL224: ##### Taipei Industrial Office MarketBeat\nL225: \nL226: * * *\nL227: \nL228: Large-scale leasing transactions reduced vacancy across NHTP in Q3, with the overall vacancy rate dropping from 11.1% to 10.3%. Vacancy fell in the Xihu, Wende and Jiuzong sections, supported by occupier expansions and relocations. Average rents remained stable across the park, although performance continued to diverge between newer and older buildings, while capital values were broadly unchanged.\nL229: 【112†Click here to download the report†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL230: ##### Taipei Retail MarketBeat\nL231: \nL232: * * *\nL233: \nL234: General merchandise retail sales rose 6.0% y-o-y in the first eight months of 2026, supported by summer spending, seasonal purchases and new mall openings. Vacancy rates across the key retail districts remained broadly stable, reflecting steady leasing demand. Garden City’s new Monet Garden retail area highlights the integration of shopping, entertainment and sports-related consumption.\nL235: \nL236: 【113†Click here to download the report†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL237: ##### Taipei Capital Markets MarketBeat\nL238: \nL239: * * *\nL240: \nL241: Quarterly transaction volume reached a record NT$105.8 billion in Q3, driven by technology sector expansion, with owner-occupier acquisitions accounting for 70% of volume. Industrial assets predominated, although the acquisition of Ruentex Yucheng Plaza highlighted renewed office investment demand. In contrast, land transaction volume totaled NT$25.6 billion, near a three-year low as developers remained cautious.\nL242: 【114†Click here to download the report†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL243: ## RELATED INSIGHTS\nL244: \nL245: ###### Article • Leisure\nL246: \nL247: 【115†What If the Next Anchor of Retail Isn’t a Store, but an Activity?】\nL248: \nL249: For decades, retail destinations have largely been shaped by what people could buy, where they could dine and which brands could attract visitors. But as wellness becomes a bigger part of everyday life, another question is becoming increasingly relevant: What can people actually do when they get there?\nL250: \nL251: 29/10/2026\nL252: ###### Insights • Economy\nL253: \nL254: 【116†2026 Autumn Budget: Implications for real estate】\nL255: \nL256: John Healey's first Budget, and the first under Andy Burnham, was always going to be about doing enough with very little room. Pre-Budget estimates suggested the £23.6bn of headroom left in March had been badly eroded, as higher inflation and bond yields ate into the buffer.\nL257: \nL258: 【117†Daryl Perry】 • 28/10/2026\nL259: \nL260: ###### Insights • Economy\nL261: \nL262: 【118†Noida: The Runway for Growth】\nL263: \nL264: 【119†Supriya Chatterjee】 • 24/11/2025\nL265: #### Download Previous Office Reports\nL266: \nL267: Click on the title to begin download:\nL268: \nL269:   * 【120†Taipei Office MarketBeat Q2 2026†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL270:   * 【121†Taipei Office MarketBeat Q1 2026†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL271:   * 【122†Taipei Office MarketBeat Q4 2025†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL272: #### Download Previous Industrial Reports\nL273: \nL274: Click on the title to begin download:\nL275: \nL276:   * 【123†Taipei Industrial MarketBeat Q2 2026†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL277:   * 【124†Taipei Industrial MarketBeat Q1 2026†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL278:   * 【125†Taipei Industrial MarketBeat Q4 2025†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL279: #### Download Previous Retail Reports\nL280: \nL281: Click on the title to begin download:\nL282: \nL283:   * 【126†Taipei Retail MarketBeat Q2 2026†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL284:   * 【127†Taipei Retail MarketBeat Q1 2026†assets.cushmanwakefield.com】\nL285:   * 【128†Taipei Retail MarketBeat Q4 2025†assets.cushma